# tools/css/check-grammars.py: what the pinned data leaves undefined, each with the reason. Every grammar of the pin is compiled
# and linked against the generated resolver (make check-css-grammars); what does not compile, and every initial value that does
# not parse, is listed here. An unlisted failure fails the gate, and so does an entry that is no longer true (the gap closed, the
# text changed, the pin raised): a listed entry is a statement about the pin and is removed with the gap.
#
# Columns (tab separated):
#   reference  <name>     the first referrer ("kind name")  the referrer's grammar  reason   a `<type>` or `<'property'>` that nothing resolves
#   grammar    kind name  -                                 the grammar's text      reason   a property, descriptor or type whose text is not a
#                                                                                           Value Definition Syntax definition, or that has none
#   initial    kind name  -                                 the initial value text  reason   an initial value that is prose, absent, or does not
#                                                                                           match the grammar of its own property
#
# A shorthand whose initial value is "see individual properties" is not listed: its initial value is its longhands'. A type the
# pin names and nothing refers to is not listed either; the references are what the gate follows.
reference	<voice-family-name>	property voice-family	[ <voice-family-name> | <generic-voice> ]# | preserve	Speech 1, 11.1 (served 2026-10-31) gives examples and no grammar for it: "Values are specific voice instances (e.g., Mike, comedian, mary, carlos2, "valley girl")." The sentence names no token form, so voice-family stays unsupported rather than guessed (GTEXT_CSS_E_UNSUPPORTED naming the property)
reference	<anchored-feature>	type anchored-in-parens	( <anchored-query> ) | ( <anchored-feature> ) | <general-enclosed>	Anchor Positioning 2 (served 2026-10-31) writes "<anchored-query> = not <anchored-in-parens> | <anchored-in-parens> [ [ and <anchored-in-parens> ]* | [ or <anchored-in-parens> ]* ] | <anchored-feature>" and defines no production named <anchored-feature> (the draft's own link check prints "No 'type' refs found" at that place); 2.3, "Anchored Container Features", describes each feature ("fallback") by itself, with no sentence that gives the type, so there is nothing to compile
reference	<boolean-expr-group>	type boolean-expr	not <boolean-expr-group> | <boolean-expr-group> [ [ and <boolean-expr-group> ]* | [ or <boolean-expr-group> ]* ]	Values 5 (served 2026-10-31) writes "<boolean-expr-group> = <test> | ( <boolean-expr[ <test> ]> ) | <general-enclosed>", a production that exists only for a <test> a using grammar supplies ("<boolean-expr[ <if-test> ]>"); the Value Definition Syntax has no parameterized types, so no one grammar is its definition
reference	<whole-value>	type cycle()	cycle( <whole-value># )	Values 5, 8.1 (served 2026-10-31): "When used as a component value of a function, <whole-value> also represents any CSS value normally valid as the whole value of the property in which it is used": it is whatever grammar the property has, so no one grammar is its definition; cycle(), first-valid() and interpolate() are read in code as pending values
